    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d994">

                <pb facs="0872" n="756" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg756"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.113/237.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Ambassador to
                        the Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.—Paraphrase.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Mexico</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-03-21T22:00:00-06:36">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 21, 1912—10 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Calero, the Minister for Foreign Affairs, has called to request the Embassy
                    to recommend that the President, in the exercise of powers conferred upon him by
                    the recent amendments to our neutrality laws, issue instructions that a shipment
                    of arms transmitted by the Mexican consul at El Paso to General Sanjinés at
                    Ojinaga and detained at Marfa, Texas, be allowed to pass the frontier. This will
                    prevent an engagement at Ojinaga on the Rio Grande and maintain a force in
                    opposition to Orozco, according to the Minister.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilson.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
